WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.360
Speaker 1: It's great to have back on the
program today, Vincent Racaniello, who's a

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:09.090
virology professor at Columbia University
and also host of This Week in Virology.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.370
Great to have you back.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:11.450
I really appreciate it.

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:12.690
Speaker 2: Oh, great to be here.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:18.630
Speaker 1: So I mean, I guess we have to start
with Macron and the if whatever prediction

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.980
you want on Macron, you can find it, which
is this is the beginning of something that's

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:23.980
going to get much worse.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:29.039
This is the beginning of the end of the pandemic
and everything in between.

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:36.670
What do we know now about the level of transmissibility
of Macron in terms of its R value, as well

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:42.020
as how much more or less likely it is to cause
hospitalization?

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:50.970
Speaker 2: So determining transmissibility
is very difficult, so getting of our value,

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:54.810
especially this far into an epidemic, is very
hard.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:01:02.300
But I like to look at what we call secondary
attack rates, which are calculated in closed

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.259
situation like a household.

00:01:04.259 --> 00:01:08.990
Yes, where you can know that this person was
infected and then within a certain amount

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.429
of time, these other people got infected.

00:01:11.429 --> 00:01:14.229
And it's not easy to do that.

00:01:14.229 --> 00:01:20.090
But recently, a preprint came out of Denmark
where they did it, and the beauty of the study

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:25.999
was it was just when they were transitioning
from Delta to Akron.

00:01:25.999 --> 00:01:31.499
So you had households that were either in
al-Muqrin household or a Delta household,

00:01:31.499 --> 00:01:32.520
it was right.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.520
Perfect, perfect.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.229
And they had good numbers and they have a
terrific medical records registry there so

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:40.650
they could get all the information.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:47.159
And what was very clear is that AMA crime
has a higher secondary attack rate than Delta,

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:51.709
so it's transmitting better in the household
situation.

00:01:51.709 --> 00:01:56.719
But that appears to be entirely due to the
fact that al-Muqrin is evading antibodies

00:01:56.719 --> 00:02:02.399
because, as you know, it has many changes
in the spike protein more than any other variant.

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:07.189
And if you take sera from people who have
been vaccinated or recovered from infection,

00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:12.440
it doesn't block infection with Macron as
well as, say, with Delta.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:20.540
And so the Danish scientists concluded that
this increased ability of al-Muqrin to transmit,

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:28.490
say, within households is entirely due to
immune evasion and not to an inherent increased

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:29.500
transmissibility of the virus.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:34.210
So we imagine that there are some properties
of viruses that can make them transmit better.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:35.560
Maybe they reproduce better.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:39.050
They're more stable in droplets that come
out of your respiratory tract.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.000
But that's not the case with Macron.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.160
It really is evading antibodies, and that's
why it's spreading now.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.540
The other part of your question how about
disease right now, with first reports out

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:57.180
of South Africa that it might be milder and
people started to call it an army cold, which

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.290
is not really right.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:02.290
My colleague Daniel Griffin, an infectious
disease doctor, says that's not right because

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:09.010
he's seen plenty of very sick people infected
with Macron and especially unvaccinated people.

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:14.320
So in the unvaccinated, Macron can be just
as severe as Delta and previous variants.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.250
But the South African study was kind of complicated
because a lot of the people in their study

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:25.610
were vaccinated and a lot of them were younger,
and a lot of them didn't have co-morbidities.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.000
And so that old bias is it to be milder.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:34.560
So, you know, making conclusions very quickly
about how severe infections are is very difficult.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.390
So I would say at this point, we know that
Macron can evade immunity, and that's why

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.210
it moves through a population.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:46.550
Well, it can infect vaccinated people just
like Delta could.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:51.390
But in a vaccinated person, the disease is
much milder than an unvaccinated person with.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.850
Speaker 1: There's so many things that we
could go off to the from there.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:55.930
Just a couple.

00:03:55.930 --> 00:04:02.290
So if we look at the UK as an example, a layperson
might look at cases and deaths.

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:07.250
And of course, at this point, we know that
not nearly all of the infections are being

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:09.920
captured as a case which depends on a test.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:15.840
But putting that aside, for a moment, it looked
like during the army, current wave cases went

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:23.600
up about six in the UK known cases, whereas
deaths went up somewhere between 80 and 100

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.040
percent or so.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:32.050
When you see that, it suggests that it is
in some way less likely to kill people and

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:33.990
that's UK, not South Africa.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:37.300
But are those two numbers alone not enough
to really tell us that?

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:42.810
Speaker 2: Well, the problem is that a lot
of the people are Vaxart are vaccinated, and

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:47.170
if you stratify the data, yes, vaccinated
versus unvaccinated, make sure you control

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:50.480
for age and comorbidities, which is not easy
to do.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:56.320
True, then you find that in its milder in
vaccinated people, but so is Delta, and so

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:57.500
are all the other variants.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:00.730
But the fact is that vaccination works.

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:02.690
It prevents you from getting severe disease.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:06.630
In that part of the issue is with Macron,
it came at about the holiday season, right

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.190
when everyone is traveling and getting together.

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:14.350
Plus, we are now getting together way more
than we ever did since the beginning of the

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:17.990
pandemic, because people are starting to relax
and all of this contributes there.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:23.220
So, you know, movement of a virus has many
factors around it, not just the virus, not

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.720
just your immunity, but how people behave
as well.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:32.850
Speaker 1: The wastewater data in some cities
in the United States is showing just dramatic

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:36.700
reductions in the amount of virus during this
Omicron wave.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:42.660
I think when I looked 10 days ago or so, or
maybe a week ago at Boston wastewater data,

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:46.860
it was down about two thirds, even though
cases are only down about 10 percent, which

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:51.400
which, you know, you would imagine that that
would be cases would be a lagging indicator

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:52.830
to the wastewater.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:54.640
How important is the wastewater data?

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:57.060
Speaker 2: I'm not sure you can.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:59.070
You can quantify anything.

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:02.900
All you can say is we have this virus here
now.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:07.660
So there may be a period where you don't see
a virus in wastewater or the levels are very

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:11.120
low and then suddenly you get more positive.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.930
So that can inform you of an impending epidemic.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:20.919
But I would not make much of the levels because,
you know, this is this is incidental virus

00:06:20.919 --> 00:06:22.220
that you're swallowing.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:26.940
It's passing out of your gastrointestinal
tract and going into the sewer.

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:27.940
So.

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:30.370
It's not really part of the infection process.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:31.370
Mm-Hmm.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:34.770
So, so to ascribe any meaning to how much
is there?

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:40.800
Remember, it's we're doing PCR to measure
viral RNA in the water.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:47.540
And as a recent study out of Switzerland just
showed, there is very little correlation between

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:49.870
PCR levels and infectious virus.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:55.889
So I think you have to be you have to realize
the limitations of looking at virus RNA in

00:06:55.889 --> 00:06:56.889
wastewater.

00:06:56.889 --> 00:06:57.889
Speaker 1: That's fair.

00:06:57.889 --> 00:06:58.889
That's fair.

00:06:58.889 --> 00:07:04.020
Last time you were on, we talked a little
bit about the very top level understanding

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:09.460
that we have about viruses over time becoming
less deadly.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:12.450
And you gave us many caveats when we talked
about that.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:17.570
But it was this sort of general idea in the
last few weeks as the conversation about what

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:21.190
happens after Macron has become a more and
more relevant conversation.

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:25.370
There have been a bunch of different articles
coming out claiming to debunk that idea as

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:33.479
a general principle that really viruses become
more chaotic and that that could get you a

00:07:33.479 --> 00:07:38.070
milder disease, but it might not, and that
there are counterexamples from history.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:41.210
Can we now because it seems more relevant
than ever?

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:45.509
Talk a little more specifically about this
idea of what is likely to come next after

00:07:45.509 --> 00:07:46.509
Macron.

00:07:46.509 --> 00:07:52.030
Speaker 2: So the idea, which we talked about
last time, you know, it's a it's a human idea

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:55.870
that viruses over time tend to get less virulent.

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:58.699
But we're talking about long, long periods
of time.

00:07:58.699 --> 00:08:00.389
We're not talking about two years here.

00:08:00.389 --> 00:08:06.930
So, for example, if a virus spilled, remember
all human viruses came from some other animal

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:09.380
at some point in time.

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:15.210
Let's say thousands of years ago, tens of
thousands of years ago, measles virus 15000

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:18.330
years ago spilled over into people from cows.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:19.330
Right.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:22.250
And so initially, it's probably quite lethal
because it's a new host.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:23.850
We have no immunity to the virus.

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:26.190
And it does what SARS-CoV-2 does.

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:32.500
But over time, population immunity grows,
and that helps you have more a milder disease

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:34.740
when you get infected now.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:39.430
There's also this hypothesis that maybe the
virus changes over time because there's no

00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:44.950
point in killing your host or making your
host very sick.

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:50.390
So random changes in the genome that reduce
virulence may persist.

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:54.730
But I have to say, you know, with the one
exception that we might have talked about

00:08:54.730 --> 00:09:02.020
last time using viruses to kill rabbits in
Australia, there's no other evidence that

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:06.810
viruses tend to get less virulent over time,
and their host is just an appealing hypothesis

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:08.180
to the Australian situation.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:12.080
They had brought in rabbits in the 1800s for
hunting purposes.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.089
They hope they multiplied like crazy.

00:09:14.089 --> 00:09:18.230
There were too many of them, so they introduced
a virus to try and kill them.

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:22.080
And in the first year, 99 percent of the rabbits
were killed.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:26.470
But then those those one percent that survived
were more resistant to the virus, and then

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:29.330
the virus changed to be less virulent as well.

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:34.490
So it's actually a beautiful experiment that's
consistent with this idea.

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:36.990
But I must say there's no other evidence for
it.

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:43.090
And as you mentioned, as soon as al-Muqrin
came out, we had these data from South Africa.

00:09:43.090 --> 00:09:47.770
People were saying, Oh, it's getting less
virulent, but I think it's far too premature

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:51.680
to say that because as we've already talked
about, there are lots of confounding factors

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:52.680
there.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:57.140
Speaker 1: So what does that do for the conversation
about what, what is next and whether we are

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:01.440
months from shifting from the pandemic to
the endemic phase of this?

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:02.440
Is it?

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:04.660
Can we really say anything about that at this
point?

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:12.010
Speaker 2: Well, from the start, we have thought
that as population immunity grew to this virus,

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:14.890
we would have less and less severe disease.

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:16.770
And we see that in vaccinated people.

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:21.070
It supports that even though we have many
variants emerging that can evade immunity

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:22.649
to a certain extent.

00:10:22.649 --> 00:10:23.870
The vaccines still work.

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:30.880
They protect you from severe disease and hospitalization,
really in the 90s percent still so.

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:36.830
And we know from other coronaviruses the common
cold coronaviruses that infect all of us every

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:43.220
year that widespread population immunity probably
associates with milder disease.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:47.830
And so that from the very start led to this
idea that over time, as more of the world

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:53.490
became immune to SARS-CoV-2, we would have
less and less severe disease.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:59.660
The virus would not necessarily be changing
with respect to virulence, but simply population

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:01.480
immunity would do that.

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:05.460
I really think that's what's going to happen
because we see the vaccines working really

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:09.260
well despite the antigenic evolution of the
virus.

00:11:09.260 --> 00:11:14.500
So I've always thought and this was actually
an idea influenced by Ralph Baric, who was

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:20.649
a corona virologist for many years, he said
this will probably be a common cold corona

00:11:20.649 --> 00:11:23.230
virus who knows how long it will take.

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:26.850
And at that point, we might not need to vaccinated
it against it any longer.

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:28.900
Speaker 1: We're not at that point yet, though.

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:33.930
Speaker 2: I don't think we are because, you
know, most of the world is still not fully

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:35.410
vaccinated, right?

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:36.410
Right.

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:41.270
There are some countries that have very high
rates of vaccination, and they have much less

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:43.820
disease as a consequence.

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:46.890
But there are other countries like Africa,
which which are barely vaccinated.

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:52.100
So we can't hope to end a pandemic until we
get well, either a lot of people infected

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:53.100
or vaccinated.

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:54.100
I mean, that's what's going to happen now.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:58.690
Over the next few years, you're going to either
get vaccinated or you're going to get infected.

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:00.930
In both cases, you're going to get some kind
of immunity.

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:05.970
Speaker 1: There are a few countries, at least
a couple, the U.K.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:10.410
Australia that are pretty quickly going in
