00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:34.980
Speaker 2: It does, and I've been thinking
about this for now many weeks because we it's

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:38.440
very clear we have multiple vaccines that
work.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:42.750
They don't prevent infection, which is normal,
but they do prevent severe disease.

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:50.120
And so in many countries like the UK and Israel,
in the U.S., where we have plenty of vaccine,

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:53.709
we now have vaccines that can control severe
disease.

00:12:53.709 --> 00:12:57.950
People who have decided not to be vaccinated
have made their choice.

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:00.980
So I think the rest of the population can
now start to move on.

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:08.200
The only problem with that idea is that if
too many unvaccinated people get infected

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:09.200
as you.

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:14.269
The health care system gets overburdened,
yes, and that is a problem because they have

00:13:14.269 --> 00:13:18.690
other issues to deal with, they have other
health problems in patients to deal with.

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:23.010
So we don't want hospitals full of unvaccinated,
severely ill COVID patients.

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:26.910
That's why I think in many places in the U.S.,
we're still masking.

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:30.589
Even though we're all fully vaccinated, many
of us are fully vaccinated.

00:13:30.589 --> 00:13:34.920
There's no risk to us anymore of getting severely
ill.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:39.850
We are protecting the unvaccinated,
Speaker 1: the biggest and most regular pushback

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:44.560
that I hear when anybody says what you just
said is, but hold on a second.

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:51.051
Even the people doing everything correctly,
getting their three doses and et cetera, they

00:13:51.051 --> 00:13:58.360
are still at greater risk due to the unvaccinated
because of the ability to spread the disease.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:05.010
The counter that's in increasingly being placed
upon us is, well, hold on a second with Anna

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:06.010
Coren.

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:09.269
It's not clear that the vaccines prevent transmission.

00:14:09.269 --> 00:14:12.550
They prevent death, they prevent serious illness.

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:14.640
But but do they really prevent transmission?

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:16.080
So it's really a two part question.

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:19.029
What about the people saying even those who
did the right things?

00:14:19.029 --> 00:14:21.610
We are still at risk because of everybody
else.

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:24.980
And number two, what do we know about the
effect of the vaccine on transmission right

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:25.980
now?

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:30.910
Speaker 2: So the I feel I am not at risk
from an unvaccinated person.

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:38.190
OK, I am triple vaccinated, and if I get infected,
which I likely will, I will have a mild disease.

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:42.060
But there's a very good chance I have a mild
disease.

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:47.959
Most of the severe disease in unvaccinated
and vaccinated people, you know that 10 percent

00:14:47.959 --> 00:14:54.140
or so that are in people with co-morbidities,
you they're older or they're obese.

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:58.040
That's a big one or a diabetic or asthmatic.

00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:02.839
Those are the people who are most likely to
have severe disease, even if fully vaccinated.

00:15:02.839 --> 00:15:06.700
Of course, we don't want to forget the kids
under five who can't be vaccinated yet.

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:09.130
We still have to be careful about them.

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:12.920
So I don't view vaccinated people as the ones
at risk.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:16.440
It's the unvaccinated people, and they're
the ones that are going to cause problems

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:17.829
in the health care system.

00:15:17.829 --> 00:15:25.110
Now, with respect to transmission, there was
a wonderful preprint out of Switzerland last

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:30.420
week and this complements other work that's
that's come out as well, which clearly shows

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:36.250
that, well, first of all, you can't measure
transmission by doing PCR.

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:41.120
That is very clear, and almost every study
that has looked at shedding of a vaccinated

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:45.450
person is wrong because they're measuring
PCR, and that does not correlate with infectious

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:47.810
virus shedding in the Swiss study.

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:53.940
They showed that a vaccinated person not only
sheds less infectious virus than an unvaccinated

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:58.630
person, but does so for fewer days after being
infected.

00:15:58.630 --> 00:16:01.579
OK, so that has significant implications.

00:16:01.579 --> 00:16:07.260
So vaccination doesn't abolish shedding, it
doesn't abolish transmission, but it knocks

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:12.570
it so far down that it no longer plays a role
in driving the epidemic.

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:15.500
So that that
Speaker 1: seems to then support those who

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:20.660
say I'm vaccinated, but the unvaccinated are
putting me at risk.

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:25.269
I know statistically you still won't be seriously
ill, but a lot of people have co-morbidities

00:16:25.269 --> 00:16:26.269
in this country.

00:16:26.269 --> 00:16:30.639
It sounds like it's not wrong to say, even
as a vaccinated person, the unvaccinated do

00:16:30.639 --> 00:16:32.170
put me at greater risk.

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:37.830
Speaker 2: No, the unvaccinated do not, because
you are not going to get sick, right?

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:41.459
If you're fully vaccinated, the likelihood
you're going to get severely ill is very low.

00:16:41.459 --> 00:16:42.459
Speaker 1: Oh, I see.

00:16:42.459 --> 00:16:43.459
So it's both.

00:16:43.459 --> 00:16:49.700
It's both that the by being by being unvaccinated,
you are shedding more virus, by being vaccinated.

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:52.139
You also are less likely to actually get sick.

00:16:52.139 --> 00:16:53.139
Speaker 2: Exactly.

00:16:53.139 --> 00:16:56.649
OK, but you are less likely to pass it on
to someone else as well.

00:16:56.649 --> 00:17:02.270
So the more people we have vaccinated, the
more likely we can stop the transmission.

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:07.079
Speaker 1: That's we are going to link to
that preprint from Switzerland.

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:10.360
It sounds like there's really a wealth of
a wealth worth of data there.

00:17:10.360 --> 00:17:15.040
We've been speaking with Vincent Racaniello,
virology professor at Columbia University,

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:17.510
and make sure to check out this week in virology.

00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:18.510
Always great having you on.

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:19.510
Speaker 2: Thank you for having me, David.

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:19.512
Appreciate it.

